      sources: "Sources: Learn, PIM integration (who can be eligible); Configure Azure resource role settings. Book 04, Appendix B. Chapter 06, “What it adds” — a desired state.",
      say: `The heart of the configuration is one reviewed file. It names the scopes, the roles, who's eligible, and the settings for each tier, taken from Book 04's role settings: shorter activations and tighter eligibility for the most sensitive tier, approval by a named group, and a justification and ticket on every activation. Those values are the program's design guidance. The loader also refuses what Learn or the program rules out. The subtle one is authentication context: Learn says to create its Conditional Access policy before you point PIM at it, so the file can't use it until that's confirmed.`,
      detail: `Read-PaAzDesiredState loads the reviewed file and refuses what Learn or the program rules out: a service principal as an eligible holder, an activation outside 1 to 24 hours, approval with no approver group, the root scope, and an authentication context before its Conditional Access policy is confirmed. That last guard is Learn's advice: create and enable the Conditional Access policy for the authentication context before you set it in PIM. The authentication context c1 and its policy are Chapter 03's (planned). The settings per tier follow Book 04, Appendix B: four hours of activation for Tier 0 and eight for Tier 1, approval by PIM-Approvers-T0 or PIM-Approvers-T1, justification and a ticket on activation, MFA and justification on an active assignment, eligibility for at most 90 or 180 days, and no permanent active assignment. Design guidance for the values. The reviewed JSON file says which scopes are governed, which roles, the settings for each tier, who is eligible, and which standing assignments are allowed and who owns each one.`,
