      sources: "Sources: Learn, PIM APIs (beta deprecation); PIM integration; role settings. Chapter 06, “Dangers.”",
      say: `Every danger here is a way a well-meant change goes wrong in production, and each has a guard in the code. The worst is order: remove standing Owner before the eligible role exists and someone loses access in the middle of a task. Close behind is the automation removing its own access halfway through a run. Others are quieter: a resource group nobody listed keeps its defaults, an authentication context points at a policy that isn't there, or a typo lands unreviewed. The pattern is the same as before: a dry run by default, and rules that run every time.`,
      detail: `A standing Owner removed before the eligible one exists: the holder loses access mid-task, or a scope is left with no one who can act; guard: eligible first, removal after, in one ordered plan; a test pins the order. The apply identity removes its own access: the run stops halfway, with policies changed and conversions half done; guard: PA-AZ-05, the caller's own standing assignment is never planned for removal. A pipeline's service principal "converted": the request fails, or the pipeline loses access it can't reactivate; guard: PA-AZ-02, reported, never planned; desired state refuses a service principal as eligible. A resource group left on default settings: activation there has no approval and lasts the default; guard: every governed scope is read and set; a test pins the resource group. An authentication context with no policy behind it: activation falls back to MFA only, or is blocked; guard: the desired state refuses c1 until its policy is confirmed. A write nobody reviewed: a typo in JSON changes a production policy; guard: dry run by default; -Apply needs an apply client; diff.txt and plan.json are the review. A rerun after a partial failure: requests repeat, and RoleAssignmentExists errors hide the real one; guard: the plan reads the estate first, so done work isn't planned again; request names are stable within a run ID. Owner instead of User Access Administrator for the apply: the automation can change every resource it governs; guard: the call list declares User Access Administrator; the manifest records the roles the task needed. Root elevation treated as a normal assignment: elevate access is converted, or quietly kept; guard: PA-AZ-03, reported for removal, never converted. The wrong cloud: a GCC High run sends tokens to management.azure.com; guard: explicit cloud, no default; the live invoker checks the Az session and the host; the desired state names its cloud. The old beta API: code built on /beta/privilegedAccess/azureResources stops returning data; guard: only the Resource Manager API at the GA version.`,
